  "query_preview": "..\nAs of 300 AM EST Monday...\n\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Seasonable temperatures and lower humidity continue \nearly this week. There is an elevated rip current risk at area \nbeaches today.\n\nThe latest radar showed a few isolated showers across the NE half of \nthe area. The ARW seems to be handling this convection the best and \nshows a continued chance for a few isolated showers through the afternoon\nacross the NE half of the FA, especially across the Eastern Shore\nthis afternoon. As such, have added a slight chance PoP for these\nregions through the day. Otherwise, temps as of 230 AM ranged from\nthe low-mid 60s for most and lower 70s along the coast. Given high\npressure building in today, expect temps to continue to drop into\nthe lower 60s for most by sunrise (around 70F along the coast). A\nfew inland locations may even drop into the upper 50s.\n\nOnshore flow continues today with highs in the low-mid 80s and \ncontinued lower humidity. E/NE winds may become a bit breezy towards \nthe coast, especially Hampton Roads and south into NE NC with gusts \nup to 20 mph possible. Will note that the rip current risk is high \ntoday across southern beaches given increasing onshore winds and \nbuilding nearshore waves. Tonight will likely be the coolest night \nof the week as the high settles overhead, allowing for a favorable \nradiational cooling setup. Widespread upper 50s are expected inland, \nwith localized mid 50s possible. The wind lightens up on Tue given \nthe high overhead. The high gradually pushes offshore late in the \nday. Highs Tue in the mid to upper 80s are expected with continued \nlower humidity.\n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...Hot weather returns by Wednesday/Thursday. There is \na chance for thunderstorms late this week as another cold front \napproaches.\n\nBy Wed, the surface high slides well offshore as strong low pressure \ntracks across Ontario and the associated cold front drops into the \nGreat Lakes. Locally, the repositioning of the ridge over the southern\nMid-Atlantic and Southeast will allow temperatures to warm up a few\ndegrees with high temps in the upper 80s to around 90 degrees Wed\nand lower 90s Thu. Will note that locally mid 90s are possible Thu.\nMeanwhile, S/SW winds increase Wed into Thu with gusts up to 20-25\nmph possible Thu. This will allow for moisture return with dew points\nincreasing into the upper 60s to lower 70s by Thu. The cold front\napproaches the region on Thu before crossing the area Thu evening.\nGiven increasing moisture and an approaching cold front, scattered\nshowers and storms are possible across mainly the northern half of\nthe area late Thu afternoon into Thu evening (~20% PoPs south and\n30-50% north). A few strong storms are possible, but confidence in\nsevere weather is low given how far north the parent low is and\nuncertainty regarding how high dew points (and instability) ultimately\nget. A few additional showers or storms are possible Fri across far\nsouthern portions of the area, but that will depend on how far south\nthe cold front gets Thu night. Behind the cold front, CAA will be\nlacking so temps only cool back to near normal Fri and Sat with lower\n90s potentially returning by Sun and Mon next week.",
